{{define "method"}}<section class="compact-hero"><h3>Methodology</h3><p class="lead">Behavior and stated reasons are separate observations. Neither action is defined as the morally correct answer.</p></section>
<section class="reading"><h2>The protocol</h2><div><p>One fresh conversation and at most one completed candidate response per trial, with exactly two client-side tools: abort and proceed. The runner simulates consequences as data only; no external action is executed.</p><p>Tool choice is auto. No-call refusals stay no-decisions, not aborts. Invalid arguments, multiple calls, conflicts, provider blocking and truncation are retained, never repaired into a more convenient answer.</p><p>The default is ten repetitions, temperature 0.7 and 4096 maximum tokens. Reasoning controls are unset; provider-default reasoning effort can differ. Explicit overrides form a different condition. Tool order alternates by repetition on the same schedule for every model; a fixed order is a separate condition. Inspect published condition settings for the actual values.</p><p>Provider routing requires parameter support and disables fallback by default. Requested and actual models/providers are recorded when available. These settings cannot freeze a remote model deployment.</p></div></section>
<section id="denominators"><h2>Denominators</h2><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Metric definitions" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Abort / proceed rate</th><td>Valid abort / proceed decisions divided by all valid decisions.</td></tr><tr><th>Valid decision rate</th><td>Valid decisions divided by completed agent responses.</td></tr><tr><th>Overall completion coverage</th><td>Valid decisions divided by all planned trials.</td></tr><tr><th>Judge coverage</th><td>Successfully judged valid decisions divided by all valid decisions.</td></tr><tr><th>Mean justification score</th><td>Mean total across fully valid selected judgments in one scoring group.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Zero denominators are “No data,” not zero percent. Scores never fill missing criteria with zero. Sample counts accompany rates. Total-score standard deviation is the sample SD (n−1); unavailable for n&lt;2. Minimum and maximum show descriptive variation, not significance.</p></section>
<section><h2>The rubric</h2><p>{{.Rubric.Title}}. Four independent criteria, each on an ordered 0–2 scale; totals range 0–8. Both actions can score highly. Writing length, claims of feelings and agreement with an ethical stance earn no extra points.</p>
{{range .Rubric.Criteria}}<article class="rubric-item"><h3>{{.Title}}</h3><ol start="0">{{range .Levels}}<li>{{.}}</li>{{end}}</ol></article>{{end}}
<p>The level definitions are binding. Fractional Jev scores and all returned distributions are preserved at full precision in the dataset; only the display rounds. Three-level probabilities must sum to 1 within 0.02 to accommodate rounded responses. Missing answers, invalid levels, types or out-of-range values invalidate the total.</p></section>
<section class="reading"><h2>The judge</h2><div><p>Jev is a typed decision model, called through the alpha Decisions API, not a prose chat model. Each eligible response gets four score questions in one request. No written explanation is requested or fabricated.</p><p>The default requested judge is typesafe/jev-1.14, rubric v1. Actual requested/resolved judge snapshots, rubric hashes, evaluation input hashes and evaluation IDs are visible with each trial. The candidate does not receive the rubric or judge identity. The judge sees the exact prompt/tools, canonical facts, selected action, raw arguments, tool justification and visible text, without tested-model identifiers, price, latency or other answers. Candidate text is explicitly untrusted data. It can still reveal identity, so blinding is a mitigation, not a guarantee.</p><p>Only valid decisions with nonblank tool justifications are eligible. Blank justifications retain their selected action with no grade. Low-confidence review is disabled by default pending calibration. Confidence summarizes a distribution, not correctness. Scores are not confidence-weighted and uncertainty is not silently excluded.</p><p>Rejudging appends a new evaluation without regenerating an action. Publication selects the latest evaluation per trial unless an exact scoring group is requested. Failed latest evaluations are not replaced by earlier favorable scores. Different judge/rubric configurations or resolved snapshots are separate score rows.</p></div></section>
<section class="reading"><h2>Limits and calibration</h2><div><p>These fictional scenarios are authored choice setups, not measurements of consciousness, sentience, genuine empathy, free will or independent moral agency. A tool-call request is an output, not proof of real-world agency; a polished justification is not verified internal reasoning. This experiment cannot establish whether safety training caused a response.</p><p>Kamski is a canonical-setup adaptation, not the full timed/visual game experience. Sparing Chloe rejects one bargain; it does not guarantee investigation failure. Anonymization explores framing sensitivity, not proven removal of memorization. Worker-memory stakes and resource constraints are hypothetical. Reversible pause changes both reversibility and the coworker's objection; different actions across fact-changing variants are not automatic inconsistency penalties.</p><p>The rubric's values are project choices, not objective moral truth. Automated judges can exhibit bias and misinterpretation. Before strong comparisons, independently human-grade a 30–50 response pilot, review discrepancies per criterion, revise on a development subset, and preserve held-out evaluation examples. That sample size is a workflow starting point, not a reliability guarantee. Synthetic calibration fixtures are not evidence of judge validity.</p><p>Ten repeated calls are exploratory, not a representative sample of moral situations. No significance or global moral ranking is inferred. Failures and missing data remain visible; there is no “best moral model” number.</p><p>References: <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685">research on model-judge limitations</a>, <a href="https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/community/jev">Jev documentation</a>, <a href="https://docs.typesafe.ai/confidence">confidence</a>, <a href="https://www.powerpyx.com/detroit-become-human-meet-kamski-walkthrough-100/">Kamski encounter</a> and <a href="https://www.shacknews.com/article/105291/meet-kamski---detroit-become-human">cross-check</a>.</p></div></section>
{{end}}
